        "target": "Given that I walk twice as fast as Mr. Harris, and Mr. Harris took 2 hours to walk to the store, I first determined the time it would take me to walk to the store. Since I walk twice as fast, it would take me half the time Mr. Harris took, which is \\(2 \\div 2 = 1\\) hour. \n\nNext, I identified that my destination is 3 times further away than the store Mr. Harris walked to. Therefore, it would take me 3 times longer to walk to this destination at the same speed. This means it would take me \\(1 \\times 3 = 3\\) hours to walk to the destination.\n\nTo verify this, I considered the relationship between speed, distance, and time. If I walk twice as fast as Mr. Harris, and the distance to the destination is 3 times the distance to the store, the time it takes me to walk to the destination is directly proportional to the distance and inversely proportional to my speed. Since my speed is twice Mr. Harris's, the time it takes me is \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\) times the time it would take Mr. Harris to walk a distance 3 times the store's distance. Given that Mr. Harris took 2 hours to walk to the store, the time it would take him to walk a distance 3 times the store's distance is \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) hours. Since I walk twice as fast, it would take me \\(\\frac{6}{2} = 3\\) hours.\n\nThe final answer is based on the direct calculation and the proportional relationship between speed, distance, and time. The concrete check is the proportional relationship I used to determine the time it would take me to walk to the destination.\n\nFINAL: 3",
