      {
        "key": "gsm8k-train-708:rich",
        "raw_call_sha256": "a07ba58834a611dfcd52e5ad8849c2a7d64b7add9af60108d4deca620e88a96f",
        "student_prefix": [
          {
            "content": "Robi Ney wants to fill a 120-liter tub. He is amused by letting the water run for 1 minute, then closing the water supply for 1 minute, and so on alternating opening and closing the water supply. But the cap at the bottom of the tub is not very airtight and lets 1 liter of water escape per minute. The flow rate of the tap is 12 liters per minute. How long does it take to fill the tub in minutes?",
            "role": "user"
          }
        ],
        "student_prefix_sha256": "fdf1fa3c2bff19179ddf63e45b4ddfe54034a4638f45be20cb146bb97f294e3e",
        "target": "Given:\n- Total volume to fill: 120 liters\n- Tap flow rate: 12 liters per minute\n- Cap leak rate: 1 liter per minute\n- Alternating 1 minute on, 1 minute off\n\nEach 2-minute cycle:\n- Water added: 12 liters (1 minute on) - 1 liter (1 minute off) = 11 liters\n\nTo find the number of full 2-minute cycles needed:\n- 120 liters / 11 liters per cycle = 10.909 cycles\n\nSince we can't have a fraction of a cycle, we need 11 full cycles to ensure the tub is filled.\n\n11 cycles * 2 minutes per cycle = 22 minutes\n\nAfter 22 minutes, 11 cycles add 121 liters, but we only need 120 liters. The tub will be filled in the next minute with the tap on, as the leak will not affect the final minute.\n\nFinal time: 22 + 1 = 23 minutes\n\nFINAL: 23",
        "target_sha256": "43236dc5c584a6ebbe39c645e14a35f4583cc570aa2d579c7ce2ca29b6522001"
      }
